## 1. Objective and scope

Determine the causal reasons why nominally identical 3D-printed polymer tensile coupons show persistent differences in tensile strength.

Specific objectives:

- Estimate how much variation is attributable to:
  - printer, build, build-plate position, and specimen;
  - feedstock condition and lot;
  - process settings and environmental conditions;
  - geometry and internal structure;
  - post-processing, conditioning, and storage;
  - tensile-test setup and laboratory conditions.
- Estimate causal effects of manipulable factors and selected interactions.
- Distinguish competing mechanisms, including:
  - bulk material degradation or composition differences;
  - poor interlayer or inter-road bonding;
  - porosity, under-extrusion, and dimensional variation;
  - thermal-history and cooling effects;
  - moisture or environmental conditioning;
  - residual stress and warpage;
  - test-machine, alignment, gripping, or operator effects.
- Develop a predictive variance budget and identify process controls that materially reduce strength differences.

Primary outcome: ultimate tensile strength calculated from independently measured load and specimen cross-section. Secondary outcomes: yield strength, modulus, strain at break, toughness, failure location, fracture orientation, and within-condition variance.

Scope should initially be restricted to one polymer formulation, coupon standard, nominal print recipe, and post-processing protocol. Generalization to other materials, geometries, and machines requires a confirmatory phase.

## 2. Assumptions

- “Nominally identical” means produced from the same digital geometry and stated process recipe, not necessarily identical in realized thermal history, dimensions, void structure, or conditioning.
- Potential outcomes are well defined after specifying treatment versions, such as actual nozzle temperature rather than only its setpoint.
- Random assignment and randomized run order make treatment groups exchangeable, subject to verified compliance.
- Coupons do not materially affect one another except through shared-machine drift or shared conditioning; such dependencies will be modeled at the batch level.
- Destructive tensile outcomes cannot be measured repeatedly on the same coupon. Comparisons therefore use randomized replicate coupons, while nondestructive measurements precede testing.
- Measurement systems are sufficiently stable after calibration, or their errors can be estimated using repeated measurements and reference artifacts.
- Printer-specific treatment effects are possible; conclusions will not assume a universal effect when only one printer is studied.
- Failure outside the gauge section may reflect a testing or specimen-validity problem and will be retained as an outcome, while standard-compliant results are analyzed separately under a prespecified rule.
- Actual process variables may mediate effects of assigned settings. Total effects of assigned interventions will be kept distinct from mechanism or mediation analyses.

A causal diagram will be specified before data collection. At minimum it will include assigned settings; printer and build; material lot and moisture; ambient conditions; realized temperature and extrusion; plate position; geometry and porosity; conditioning; test machine and operator; alignment and gripping; and strength and failure mode.

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Measurement-system qualification

Before studying manufacturing effects:

1. Calibrate load, displacement/extensometry, temperature, humidity, and dimensional instruments.
2. Conduct gauge repeatability and reproducibility studies across operators, machines, and measurement sessions.
3. Use reference specimens or a stable nonprinted material to test whether tensile-machine, grip, alignment, and operator differences generate apparent strength differences.
4. Blind specimen identifiers during dimensional measurement, imaging, fracture classification, and tensile testing where practical.

Proceed only if measurement error is small relative to the strength differences of scientific interest or can be modeled reliably.

### Phase B: Baseline variance decomposition

Produce coupons under a tightly documented nominal recipe using a crossed, blocked design:

- multiple qualified printers;
- multiple independent builds per printer;
- multiple plate positions per build;
- multiple feedstock lots and, where feasible, independently prepared spools from each lot;
- randomized assignment of specimen orientation and plate position;
- randomized tensile-test order, operator, and test machine;
- balanced delay between printing, conditioning, and testing.

Replicate across days rather than printing all specimens in one session. Include within-build replicates to estimate local variation and between-build replicates to estimate drift.

This phase estimates variance components and identifies whether persistent differences cluster by printer, build, plate region, material lot, test session, or specimen.

### Phase C: Randomized causal interventions

Use a sequential factorial design, informed by Phase B but specified before examining outcome associations in detail. Randomize manipulable factors within appropriate blocks. Candidate factors include:

- feedstock moisture: controlled dry versus controlled humid condition;
- material lot;
- actual nozzle temperature target;
- build-platform temperature;
- chamber temperature or enclosure condition;
- cooling-fan setting;
- print speed;
- volumetric flow/extrusion multiplier;
- layer height and road width;
- raster orientation and perimeter strategy;
- coupon orientation on the plate;
- plate position;
- nozzle condition or controlled nozzle replacement;
- post-print annealing;
- conditioning duration and humidity.

Use a fractional factorial screening design if the factor count is large, while protecting main effects and prespecified two-way interactions from aliasing. Follow with a full factorial or response-surface experiment for factors showing material effects. Randomization must occur at the level where intervention is feasible:

- printer/build-level treatments, such as chamber temperature, are cluster-randomized;
- coupon-level treatments, such as plate position, are randomized within builds;
- post-processing treatments may be randomized within a build;
- test-machine and operator assignments are randomized after manufacture.

Include periodic center-point or reference-recipe builds to detect temporal drift. Cross printer, material lot, and important process factors where feasible so printer effects are not confounded with lot or day.

### Phase D: Diagnostic interventions to distinguish mechanisms

Conduct targeted experiments based on prespecified contrasts:

- **Moisture/degradation:** randomize feedstock drying, then measure moisture, molecular-weight proxies, and strength. A drying effect accompanied by restored material properties supports this explanation.
- **Interlayer bonding:** vary thermal conditions while holding geometry and mass approximately constant; compare specimens loaded across versus along layer interfaces. Effects concentrated in cross-layer loading and interface-following fractures support bonding failure.
- **Porosity/under-extrusion:** randomize controlled extrusion and speed settings; measure mass and internal void fraction before testing. Strength changes tracking void fraction support this explanation.
- **Thermal history/crystallinity:** manipulate cooling, chamber temperature, or annealing; measure crystallinity and residual stress proxies. Strength changes with these properties after geometry is held fixed support this mechanism.
- **Testing artifacts:** distribute coupons from each build across machines, operators, grips, and test order. Effects that follow the test station rather than manufacturing provenance support a measurement explanation.
- **Plate-position thermal gradients:** repeatedly randomize coupon locations while mapping surface or chamber temperature. Reproducible spatial patterns that move with location rather than coupon identity support local process heterogeneity.

Where practical, use same-build matched coupons for destructive microscopy and tensile testing so mechanism evidence and strength outcomes share treatment history.

### Controls and safeguards

- Freeze digital files, slicing software version, firmware, and parameter definitions.
- Archive machine logs and raw sensor streams.
- Use unique coded identifiers and complete chain-of-custody records.
- Prespecify exclusions, transformations, primary contrasts, minimum effect size, and analysis code structure.
- Do not adjust total-effect models for post-treatment mediators such as porosity or crystallinity; examine them in separate mediation analyses.
- Determine replication through simulation or power calculations based on the smallest practically important strength difference and expected clustering.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

For every coupon:

- material manufacturer, formulation, lot, spool, color/additives, receipt date, and storage history;
- measured feedstock moisture immediately before use;
- printer, nozzle, maintenance history, firmware, slicer version, and calibration status;
- assigned and realized process settings;
- timestamped nozzle, bed, chamber, and ambient temperature and humidity;
- commanded and measured extrusion where available;
- build identifier, run order, plate coordinates, coupon orientation, and neighboring objects;
- coupon mass, external dimensions, cross-sectional area, surface roughness, and warpage;
- internal void fraction, void morphology, road geometry, and dimensional defects for a representative or fully scanned subset;
- conditioning environment, duration, post-processing, storage, and test delay;
- test machine, load cell, grips, operator, alignment checks, test speed, extensometer method, and specimen order;
- raw force, displacement, strain, and time data;
- calculated mechanical properties with traceable processing steps;
- failure location, fracture angle, visible defect origin, and coded fracture morphology.

For mechanism assessment, obtain microscopy of fracture surfaces and sections; thermal-analysis measurements relevant to crystallinity or transitions; and, if degradation is plausible, an appropriate molecular-weight or rheological proxy. Preserve untested witness coupons and feedstock samples from each key condition.

Document deviations, failed prints, invalid tests, and missing data rather than silently excluding them.

## 5. Measurement

### Outcomes

- Ultimate tensile load.
- Ultimate tensile strength using measured initial gauge-section area.
- Yield strength under a prespecified definition.
- Elastic modulus from extensometer strain.
- Strain at break and toughness.
- Failure location and mode.
- Probability of invalid grip or shoulder failure.

Report both load-based and stress-based outcomes. A difference in tensile load with no difference after area normalization suggests geometry; a difference remaining after normalization suggests material or structural quality.

### Exposures and mediators

Treat assigned interventions as primary exposures. Record realized settings as compliance measures. Potential mediators include:

- cross-sectional area and mass;
- pore volume and orientation;
- inter-road and interlayer contact quality;
- crystallinity and thermal history;
- moisture at printing and testing;
- degradation proxies;
- residual stress and warpage.

### Measurement-quality procedures

- Calibrate instruments using traceable standards.
- Repeat dimensional measurements by blinded operators.
- Quantify uncertainty in cross-sectional area, particularly for rough or nonrectangular printed surfaces.
- Use an extensometer rather than crosshead travel for strain where possible.
- Verify grip alignment and strain-rate compliance before each test block.
- Photograph specimens before and after testing using standardized lighting and scale.
- Classify fracture mode independently by at least two blinded raters and resolve disagreements under a prespecified rubric.
- Retain raw data and calculate outcomes through a version-controlled pipeline.

## 6. Analysis

### Estimands

Primary estimands:

- intention-to-treat average causal effect of each randomized process intervention on tensile strength;
- printer-, lot-, and orientation-specific treatment effects;
- change in between-build and within-build variance under improved control;
- causal effect of test-system assignments on reported strength.

Per-protocol or dose-response effects based on realized process variables will be secondary because noncompliance and sensor error may be informative.

### Models

Use hierarchical models reflecting the design, for example:

- fixed effects for randomized interventions and prespecified interactions;
- random effects for printer, material lot, build, plate region, test session, and specimen;
- random slopes when treatment effects may vary across printers or lots;
- heteroskedastic residuals if interventions alter variability as well as mean strength.

Estimate variance components with uncertainty intervals. Analyze invalid-test probability and fracture category separately using suitable generalized hierarchical models.

For factorial screening, control the false discovery rate for exploratory contrasts while retaining unadjusted estimates and intervals for prespecified primary contrasts. Report effect sizes in engineering units and relative to the minimum practically important difference, not only p-values.

### Causal identification

- Randomized contrasts identify total effects under assignment integrity and consistency.
- Cluster-level effects require cluster-level replication; coupons within one build do not substitute for independent builds.
- Observational sensor associations will be treated as hypothesis-generating unless supported by randomization, valid quasi-random variation, or justified adjustment using the causal diagram.
- If randomization is compromised, estimate effects using documented assignment probabilities and sensitivity analyses rather than relying on naïve regression.

### Mechanism and competing-explanation tests

Use the joint pattern of intervention, mediator, and failure observations:

- **Material-composition/degradation explanation:** lot or conditioning interventions alter strength across print orientations, accompanied by material-property changes and predominantly bulk rather than interface failure.
- **Interlayer-bonding explanation:** thermal interventions have larger effects in cross-layer loading; fracture follows layer boundaries; interfacial defects improve without a comparable change in bulk reference properties.
- **Porosity/geometry explanation:** extrusion-related interventions change mass, area, or void structure; strength differences attenuate when comparing equal effective area or void fraction. Mediation estimates quantify, but do not prove, the pathway.
- **Thermal-history explanation:** chamber, cooling, or annealing interventions alter crystallinity or residual-stress measures and produce the predicted mechanical changes.
- **Testing-artifact explanation:** apparent differences follow machine, grip, operator, alignment, or test order and are reproduced in reference specimens.
- **Printer-identity explanation:** a printer effect persists after crossed calibration, common material, randomized settings, and common testing, suggesting unmeasured hardware or control-system differences requiring further intervention.

Evaluate whether each explanation predicts the observed direction, orientation dependence, spatial pattern, fracture mode, and response to intervention. Avoid declaring mediation solely because adjustment reduces a coefficient.

### Robustness analyses

- Analyze load and stress using alternative area definitions.
- Include and exclude standard-invalid failures under prespecified estimands.
- Examine missingness and failed-print rates as outcomes.
- Test sensitivity to influential builds, lot imbalance, drift, and plausible measurement error.
- Confirm major findings in a fresh set of builds, ideally using held-out printers, days, and material lots.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Define in advance:

- a minimum practically important difference in tensile strength;
- an acceptable upper bound for residual between-build variation;
- required precision for primary causal effects;
- acceptable measurement-system contribution to total variance.

A factor will be considered a supported cause when:

1. it was randomized or otherwise credibly identified;
2. the estimated effect is practically important and its uncertainty excludes effects too small to matter;
3. the result replicates across independent builds and is not driven by one printer, lot, or test session; and
4. predicted diagnostic evidence is observed when a specific mechanism is claimed.

A competing explanation will be disfavored when a sufficiently precise, well-powered intervention produces neither the predicted strength effect nor its prespecified diagnostic pattern. Absence of statistical significance alone will not be treated as evidence of no effect.

Stop the screening phase when all primary factors have sufficient independent replication and uncertainty targets are met. Advance to confirmation when one or more interventions materially reduce either mean deficits or variance. Conclude practical equivalence only if the entire compatibility interval lies within the prespecified equivalence bounds.

Stop the overall study when a blinded confirmatory set demonstrates both:

- reproducible causal effects or equivalence for the principal factors; and
- a process-control recipe that reduces residual variability below the predefined engineering threshold.

Allow early stopping only for equipment safety, severe protocol failure, or overwhelming evidence under a prespecified sequential boundary—not after unplanned inspection of favorable results.

## 8. Limitations

- Findings may be specific to the chosen polymer, formulation, geometry, slicing strategy, printer class, and tensile standard.
- Some interventions cannot be randomized at coupon level, limiting effective sample size.
- Internal imaging and chemical characterization may be feasible only for a subset, weakening mediation precision.
- Realized nozzle and interface temperatures are difficult to measure without perturbing the process.
- Strength is destructive, so coupon-level changes over time cannot be observed directly.
- Interactions may be numerous; fractional factorial designs can miss higher-order effects.
- Statistical mediation relies on stronger assumptions than randomized total-effect estimation.
- Instrument and cross-sectional-area errors can bias calculated stress even when load is accurate.
- Excluding malformed prints can create selection bias; including them may answer a different production-quality question. Both should therefore be reported.
- Short-term experiments may not capture machine wear, spool aging, seasonal humidity, or long-term storage effects.
- A stable observed printer effect does not by itself identify its internal mechanism; additional hardware-specific interventions may be required.

